{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1550009-gurahl-mechanical-deep-dive","id":1550009,"title":"Gurahl (Mechanical Deep Dive)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-04-10T11:22:52-07:00","id":1550009,"title":"Gurahl (Mechanical Deep Dive)","content":"<span style=\"font-size:26px\"><i><b>Werebears!</b></i></span> The Gurahl are Gaia's healers, chosen to protect the living and to fight disease. They have animal kin among the various bear species, and their numbers were hugely impacted by the War of Rage, in which they were among the most impacted victims.<br /> <br /> In this thread I'll examine the mechanics of the Gurahl, comparing them to other Fera and most especially to Garou.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Overview</b></u><ul><li>All Gurahl start with a base Willpower of 6; they're very stubborn and changing their minds is extremely difficult. And this is compared to a Garou. Gurahl get +1 success on Opposed Willpower rolls relating to defending and protecting, anything attempting to control their mind or possess them suffers a +2 difficulty, and finally when they are in a contest of wills with another over serving Gaia they receive an advantage determined by the Storyteller.</li> <li>Gurahl enjoy a -2 difficulty to all scent-based sensory rolls in all forms but Homid, and in all forms except Homid they suffer a +2 difficulty to all entirely sight-based sensory rolls.</li> <li>Werebears reduce their Initiative score by 2 in Winter months.</li> <li>All Gurahl must buy Mentor 1, as a minimum, and the Kinfolk Background grants fewer Kinfolk.</li> <li>Gurahl have access to all five of their Auspices, moving through them across longer periods of time, driven by changes in their character and story.</li> <li>Every Gurahl has an aspect of Bear as their Totem, for free, gaining a relevant benefit. This is +1 Survival, along with an option of (<u><b>Cub:</b></u> +1 Expression and +1 Larceny), (<u><b>She-Bear:</b></u> +1 Empathy and +1 Expression), or (<u><b>Death-Bear:</b></u> +1 Brawl and +1 Enigmas); as with Auspices a Gurahl may shift between different faces of Bear as they go through their life.</li> <li>Gurahl cannot Step Sideways by default, but have a few ways of crossing the Gauntlet, most especially Umbral Glens and a purpose-made Rite.</li> </ul><u><b>Rage and Frenzy</b></u><br /> <br /> Gurahl may spend Rage for a few additional purposes, compared to other Fera:<ul><li>1 Rage for +1 Strength (no higher than double their current form's original Strength) lasting for 1 turn per permanent Dot of Rage</li> <li>1 Rage for +1 Stamina (no higher than double their current form's original Stamina) lasting for 1 turn per permanent Dot of Rage</li> <li>1 Rage for an additional Health Level lasting for one scene, any health level gained will based on your current level of wounds, so if uninjured you'll gain additional Bruised health levels</li> </ul>As a tradeoff they don't regain Rage from things which would impact most other Fera; a threat to Gaia's sanctity would restore Rage while a fierce argument would not.<br /> <br /> Gurahl very rarely Frenzy; their Rage roll to Frenzy is difficulty 8, and they need 5 successes, or 8 for Thrall.<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that W20 doesn't explicitly say that Gurahl can spend Rage for bonus actions, but it also never says or implies, as far as I can see, that they don't get bonus actions from Rage.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Bhernocht</b></u><br /> <br /> Akin to Harano, this is a state where a Gurahl willingly fails to uphold their duties. They lose Willpower continuously until they fall into hibernation, and they also lose all the benefits they'd typically enjoy regarding Willpower defenses and rolls. While this is unlikely to come up in play for a typical PC in a typical session, it's worth keeping this in mind. A Gurahl simply can't refuse to attempt to carry out their duties, if they do the impact is severe and ramps up very swiftly over a course of days.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Breeds</b></u><br /> <br /> Gurahl may attempt to reproduce with one another, but this always fails; either the pairing is sterile or the child will be stillborn. Since conception is possible, given that a stillbirth can occur, it seems feasible that a Metis Gurahl could come into existence through powerful interference, but for all intents and purposes Gurahl are divided into Homid and Ursine.<br /> <br /> Homid Gurahl start with a Rage of 3 and a Gnosis of 4.<br /> <br /> Ursine Gurahl suffer the typical Lupus drawbacks regarding Backgrounds and Abilities are character creation, and start with a Rage of 4 and a Gnosis of 5.<br /> <br /> Much like with Garou, the animal Breed does have a significant advantage... but when all is said and done the difference is much, much smaller for Gurahl. A difference of 3 freebie points worth of traits is pretty negligible.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Auspices</b></u><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Instead of having one \u201cbirth\u201d auspice that governs an individual throughout his lifetime, Gurahl migrate through five different auspices in the course of their lives. There is a natural progression of auspices, and the vast majority of Gurahl transition through them in that order. In more peaceful times, a Gurahl would take an entire lifetime to experience all five auspices. In modern times, however, many Gurahl rush through all the auspices in a few years, finally settling on the one most suitable to their temperament - or most necessary to the responsibilities they\u2019ve taken on - as a long-term auspice. This sometimes means going back to a previous auspice, or remaining in one for an extended time period, if duty requires it. </div> </div> </div><b>Arcas</b> is the Auspice of the New Moon, and it reflects a time of learning, exploration and growth. You might think of it like the Fool Tarot, it's a starting point full of potential. Typically a Gurahl advances to Uzmati when they enter a period of solitary travel, leaving their first mentor for a time.<br /> <br /> <b>Uzmati</b> is the Auspice of the Full Moon, reflecting a stronger draw towards battle. While in this Auspice a Gurahl is expected to be more of a defender and take on a greater burden in times of war. As a result some Gurahl stay in this Auspice for a very long time (which makes sense with the Apocalypse on the horizon) and some leave it very swiftly, having little taste for battle. Kojubat begins when a Gurahl mates, marries, or settles down into a more reflective role.<br /> <br /> <b>Kojubat</b> is the Auspice of the Gibbous Moon, and it handles lore and understanding. Well respected for tending the knowledge Gaia imparted upon the Gurahl, this phase tends to end when the werebear turns towards teaching.<br /> <br /> <b>Kieh</b> is the Auspice of the Crescent Moon, focused on mentoring, mysticism and spiritual matters. Finally, when a Gurahl reaches a position where their wisdom is called upon by others to settle disputes, they reach the final phase.<br /> <br /> <b>Rishi</b> is the Auspice of the Half Moon, focused on leadership, judgement, arbitration and peace-keeping.<br /> <br /> A Gurahl can move abnormally swiftly through this rotation, and later return to old Auspices, or even repeatedly go through the cycle. Traditionally it was deemed wise to go through all 5 in order, very strictly, and then eventually settle on your 'true' role in later life. Given genocide and apocalyptic chaos, Gurahl just figure things out as they go these days.<br /> <br /> The Totem a werebear is currently affiliated with is also loosely connected to their current Auspice, so a Gurahl deep in Rage and despair in their solemn wandering as an Uzmati would likely find a closer kinship with Mangi, the Death-Bear, while a Gurahl who enters Uzmati while keeping their innocence might cling onto Ursa Minor.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Forms</b></u><br /> <br /> Gurahl utilize 5 forms, <b>Homid</b>, <b>Arthren</b>, <b>Crinos</b>, <b>Bjornen</b>, and <b>Ursus</b>.<br /> <br /> This post isn't a substitute for reading W20 Changing Breeds. Suffice it to say that Gurahl are very, very strong and sturdy in their Crinos, enjoy a +5 bonus to Strength and Stamina, along with a -1 penalty to Dexterity.<br /> <br /> <u><b>First Thoughts</b></u><br /> <br /> Before getting into Gifts (which are split into General, Breed and Auspice), it's worth noting that the Gurahl are very flexible. They have 5 Auspices which you can move vaguely freely through, bound more by the tides of the story than the laws of the book. Naturally a hyper-optimized path between the Auspices won't fly at a good table, but you shouldn't want that. Just relax and remember that if you desperately need some Gifts from a certain Auspice, chances are you'll find yourself working your way back to that Auspice.<br /> <br /> The immediate potential for a high-Rage Gurahl to temporarily have a Strength or Stamina of 20, while in Crinos, is extremely appealing in itself. Gurahl lack a few Garou advantages, but they make up for what they lose with a lot of powerful bonuses. So before getting into Gifts, the basekit is... perhaps slightly better. If I had the option to move my Child of Gaia Metis through the Auspices, flowing between them over the years as he matures and changes and faces different challenges, I'd immediately take it, even if it were a 7 point Merit or some other huge investment. Gurahl promises a character who could, if they weren't in a team-building game where a 'pack' of some kind comes together, probably manage alone. Such characters can, with thought, function amazingly in a team. You could be the generalist, or specialize in something important (like combat) and then shift into something else (like being a face or mystic).<br /> <br /> Unfortunately the big problem for Gurahl is bonus actions. W20 isn't crystal clear about this, but across the history of Werewolf my impression is that Gurahl don't get to spend Rage for additional actions. This may be the intent in W20; in W20 corebook we are told that Gurahl spend Rage &quot;differently&quot; before we're given their special uses, implying that they might not get the standard uses. Presumably they can still use Rage to transform and ignore stunning, and the RAI seems to be that you can't get bonus actions.<br /> <br /> This is a huge downside, and somewhat unique. Gurahl and Nuwisha are alone in lacking bonus actions, to the best of my knowledge, and it really does suck both defensively and offensively. Do the Gurahl's unique options make up for it? I'd say, all in all, no. If you have a very high Rage and you wind up with a Stamina of 20... well, then you're somewhat unassailable. But if you can't produce enough Rage to get the locomotive going, you're going to suffer. With all this in mind, a Garou (or rough equivalent like Ajaba) is generally going to fare better. But in a team? You can be the slow burn powerhouse who gradually heats up until you are unstoppable.<br /> <br /> The other big change is that you can't typically Step Sideways. To do so you need the Step Sideways Merit, or the Rite of Rending the Gauntlet, or to find an Umbral Glen. This is a negotiable problem, especially if you have the correct Rite, but should be kept in mind.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 05-02-2025, 06:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-10T13:29:25-07:00","id":1550011,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Green</b></span> Gifts are often must-haves, or have applications which are incredibly game-changing, story-impacting or overall powerful. <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Amber</b></span> Gifts are solid, their utility is often determined by how likely you are to need the given effect or whether it's important to your character concept. <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Red</b></span> Gifts are, sadly, too weak to typically be a good investment. A Red Gift might have an insane cost for its effect, or have too hard a roll to reliably pass, or be entirely redundant, and so on.<br /> <br /> See my Garou thread for details on pre-existing Gifts.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking#post1511902\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/filedata/fetch?filedataid=1074\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking#post1511902\"> Garou Gifts: Ranking, Thinking, Overthinking - Onyx Path Forums </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking#post1511902\"> https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking#post1511902 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> I'm going to break down Gifts by level and category, bit by bit. If it's Wyrmish, Fera-only, obscure or lost it's going to feature much much later, if at all. This is intended to focus on the Gifts you will actually be getting in play, hence starting at level 1 and working up. I'll be considering utility (both inside and </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <u><b>General Gurahl Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Desperate Strength</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Healing Tongue (Mother's Touch)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Ignore Wounds (Resist Pain)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Sense Pattern Breaker (Sense Wyrm)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Ursa's Cleansing (Resist Toxin)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Wyld Resurgence</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Calm</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Grisly Aspect (True Fear)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Roused to Fury (Para Bellum)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Calm the Savage Beast</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Ease the Fevered Mind (Lover's Touch)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Survival of the Bear (Adaptation)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Heart of the Mountain</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Hide the Bear (Bury the Wolf)</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Nature's Plenty (1):</b></u></span> Find food and herbs necessary to look after someone, no matter where you are or in what conditions you're working.<br /> <br /> This costs Gnosis and requires a difficulty 7 Perception + Survival; given that you will likely reduce the difficulty to 5 (good scent) and you get +1 Survival from the Bear Totem, this is very easy to pull off. The addition of herbs, as well as food, could make this decently handy in isolation. I maintain, as always, that a supernaturally enhanced superbeing can find lunch. And given that you have Mother's Touch, you don't have much call for mundane herbs in most scenarios.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sentinel's Warning (1):</b></u></span> This is one of those Gifts which is just lovely in how absolute it is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Storyteller warns the character when danger is imminent (though not the nature of the threat) one turn before it appears. This Gift\u2019s effects are permanent. </div> </div> </div>No cost, no rolls, you just always know a second before danger shows up. Good moment to boost your Stamina or Health Levels or yell to those under your protection to get down. This is a character-defining Gift which could save your party countless times. As far as we can tell there is no way around this Gift, so truly ambushing you will be nigh-impossible.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Treeshake (2):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, shake a tree, get enough food to feed (Succour) people. The lack of a roll is nice, feeding a number of people is nice... but you have superpowers, you can find lunch. Like almost all Gifts of this ilk, this is very weak.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Dreams of the Buri-Jaan (3):</b></u></span> Spend 1-2 Gnosis and roll Wits + Occult (difficulty 4-9); on a success you can send dreams to a Gurahl you wish to guide and mentor. 1 Gnosis covers a cub you know and mentor, 2 Gnosis covers any Gurahl cub who wishes to learn. The difficulty is based on distance.<br /> <br /> This is mostly a story-based power which will be used by mentoring NPCs. In all honesty you could make this a core part of the Gurahl, a free benefit of being a Buri-Jaan, or a Rite, and things would be fine. This is basically a 9xp &quot;be someone's Mentor&quot; tax, and I'd gladly give this Gift for 0xp to relevant PCs.<br /> <br /> Purely as a 'power' you can pick up for your character this is pretty poor.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Masking the Hunted (4):</b></u></span> Use natural terrain to hide up to 12 individuals, who can each have a size up to that of a bear. Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Manipulation + Stealth (difficulty 7), and this lasts for 1 hour per success.<br /> <br /> I wish this was Survival, not Stealth, but the overall effect of the Gift is pretty neat. The annoying thing is that this lets you conceal creatures using the natural terrain. This is good for hiding, but it's far from an invisibility power, and at Rank 4 you're expecting proper invisibility powers, not a clever den. I could easily imagine pulling something like this off with a relatively weak Fetish. You could also easily remove the Gnosis cost, or the roll, or just set the duration to Succour hours, and so on.<br /> <br /> All in all this is handy if you need to hide a whole party using natural terrain... but at this Rank you expect someone to have a Gift or Fetish which does this better. Effecting up to 12 characters is handy, absolutely, but the difficulty 7 roll and the Gnosis cost mean this is far from free or reliable.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Gaia's Breath (5):</b></u> This Gift is hugely important to the Gurahl in terms of role and lore. It lets you revive a shapechanger, just the once.<br /> <br /> Spend 1 permanent Gnosis and 1 permanent Willpower, then roll Charisma + Occult (base difficulty 6, +1 for every hour since death). A single success restores them to life with 1 health level restored, additional successes remove more levels of damage.<br /> <br /> Whether you succeed or fail, you can only attempt this once, ever, on an individual. So you can only restore a dead Garou ally once, if she dies again she's gone forever. Oh and a botch means a powerful Bane possesses the corpse.<br /> <br /> This is very, very good, and could do a lot to circumvent terrible situations in which recovery would otherwise be impossible. The loss of Gnosis and Willpower is a significant xp sink however! It's also worth noting that this Gift doesn't always compare favourably to the Rite of Fighting the Death Bear, which costs 0xp to acquire and doesn't cost any Gnosis or Willpower.<br /> <br /> As a result, while Gaia's Breath is very good, I'm leaving it unrated.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Gentle Soul (5):</b></u></span> Ignore the drawbacks of The Curse, passively.<br /> <br /> The problem is that this Gift is inferior to <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Beyond Human</b></span>, which flips The Curse from a drawback into a positive, as well as granting the ability to buff your Social Attributes with Gnosis. I'd be very quick to replace Gentle Soul with Beyond Human, or some version of it. My version of Gentle Soul would permit the Gurahl to spend Gnosis to buff their Social Attributes, and invert The Curse so that it makes them come across as a creature of wisdom and importance, instead of Beyond Human's effect of making you seem alluring and intriguing. <hr /><br /> Universal access to Mother's Touch, Resist Pain and Sense Wyrm adds up to a great mechanical core. Sentinel's Warning is incomparable and outweighs the whole lists of some Tribes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 04-11-2025, 03:03 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-11T05:03:50-07:00","id":1550032,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Homid Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Master of Fire</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Persuasion</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Dolorous Countenance (Staredown)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Shape Matter (Reshape Object)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Spirit Shield (Spirit Ward)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Ursa's Coat (Cocoon)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Fearless Unveiling (Part the Veil)</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Ursa's Light (1):</b></u></span> No cost, reach towards the sky, make a Charisma + Occult roll (difficulty 6), you can fill a 100/200 foot area with a soft light, or make a 100/200 yard directional beacon; if you can see Ursa Major or Minor you get the higher number.<br /> <br /> The lack of a cost, at Rank 1, for such an obviously supernatural ability is very funny to me. A Homid Gurahl can just spam this ability. But is it useful? Well, a 3xp lighting option which will fill a huge area is actually pretty neat. I'd wager you get get a similar effect with a 1 point Fetish, but I'd say this is probably a worthwhile investment of 3xp. That said you could skip it and possibly never miss it. If the light had a rider effect, like helping find individuals who are carrying disease or madness (e.g. Black Spiral Dancers) then this would be much improved.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Climate Control (2):</b></u></span> W20 uses both celcius and farenheit, and there isn't a 'measures' portion where it establishes which it prefers. As a result this Gift either adjusts the temperature by 5 degrees C, or F, and we don't know which it is. Given that +5 degrees celcius is equal to +9 degrees farenheit, this does matter!<br /> <br /> Let's assume celcius, because we're all sane. No cost, roll Manipulation + Primal-Urge, difficulty 6, and each success lets you increase or lower the local ambient temperature by 5 degrees.<br /> <br /> So, for example, you could go from 20 degrees up to 50 degrees, in a micro-climate, by spamming this Gift until animals start falling out of the trees dead. I'm very glad that the servants of the Wyrm don't have access to this Gift. &quot;If Gaia had given this Gift to Garou, your Spiral Dancers would have melted the ice caps by now.&quot; &quot;<i>Our </i>dancers? You'll die for those words bear!&quot;<br /> <br /> How good is significantly warming up or cooling down an area? It's handy for healing, keeping sick people warm is very important. It's good for roleplay and for comfort. It could help solve puzzles, like in a mysterious umbral temple you could freeze all the water making new areas accessible, which is very Zelda. It could save your life in extreme conditions. It could help you keep a rare plant or animal alive. You could also mess with humans non-violently, driving them away by making it too cold for their Veil-risking picnic.<br /> <br /> All that said... for 6xp this is definitely worth it, but not a game-changer.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Need (3):</b></u></span> This is the Superman Gift, essentially. Reach out and feel for people in a pretty big radius (Succour miles) who need help or rescue. Perception + Empathy, difficulty 6, and more successes gives you a more clear and determined pull, with the word 'clarity' implying that you get some kind of impression of what vague category of help you might provide.<br /> <br /> In a mixed-Fera game this makes you a bit like the first responder to an emergency call, you can triage, decide who goes where, and so on. In Downtime this Gift could help you to save a lot of ordinary humans from the work of Banes, Pentex and so on, and it could also pull you towards cubs in danger. This Gift being used would be a great way to introduce a Gurahl NPC for example.<br /> <br /> Honestly I'd just bump this over to the General Gurahl list, but, as it stands, this is amazing. If Children of Gaia could learn this Gift I'd absolutely love to get it on my boy, and a Fetish which does something similar would be ace. No cost and it opens up infinite possibilities.<br /> <hr /><br /> The immediate problem is that the Garou Homid list is incredible, that if you're playing a Garou or Ajaba or even a Bastet you'll have a great Gift list if you're a Homid, and this is... pretty bleak. 10 Gifts total, and they're pretty bad as a cohort. I'd strongly suggest giving Homid Gurahl the Homid Garou list and removing a few Gifts which would feel off (City Running doesn't feel slow and ponderous, for example). But, RAW, the Gurahl don't get much from this Breed list. It's not only bad for Homid Gurahl, it's bad for Ursine, who have fewer out-of-breed options they can easily purchase.<br /> <br /> Oh and as an aside... can I say how horrible most of the renames of Gifts in W20 are? Changing Staredown to &quot;Dolorous Countenance&quot; sounds like someone swallowed a thesaurus. I love using cool and interesting words, but you can use those when you're naming the Gifts in the first place. Only bother renaming a Gift for IMPORTANT lore reasons, like replacing Wolf with Bear or changing the name of a major spirit like the Weaver to fit the culture.<br /> <br /> And finally, isn't it really weird that Gurahl don't get Grandmother's Touch? That's weird right? Just add it to their General list at Level 2.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-12T03:03:56-07:00","id":1550065,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Ursine Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Create Element</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Heightened Senses</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Burrow</b></span><br /> (No pre-existing Level 3 Gifts)<br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Gnaw</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Sweet Swarm of Vengeance (Infest)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Bear's Bounty (Totem Gift)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Call of the Cave Bear (Song of the Great Beast)</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Voice of Woe (1):</b></u></span> This Gift is a distress beacon, essentially. Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Charisma + Primal-Urge (difficulty 6), all Gaian shapeshifters within successes x 5 miles know who's in trouble, why, and what direction to go to get there. This Gift makes me sad. Because what immediately comes to mind is that all Garou, save for BSDs, will hear the call of this Gift. Gurahl will have cried for help only to draw in their killers in some cases.<br /> <br /> In a typical game you can expect to use this to summon other PCs, and friendly NPCs. Just be mindful that rivals and outright enemies among Gaia's shapeshifters will be drawn in. Also keep in mind that if there is an Ananasi PC, or NPCs, they won't be able to hear this Gift's cry, as they aren't &quot;Gaian&quot; in origin!<br /> <br /> The cost is a little annoying, but having a handy distress beacon which will typically reach 5-15 miles and bring in a few Fera to help you, in many games, is great. There is an edge case where this Gift helps reveal a non-Gaian shapeshifter for what they truly are; a Gangrel or Fomori or Mage or Changeling who has momentarily come across as a &quot;werewolf&quot; because they turned from a human into a wolf won't be able to hear this cry, and a BSD won't be able to hear it either.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Weather Watch (2):</b></u></span> +5 dice, passively, on all rolls relating to predicting the weather. Boo. Short of weird Umbral situations this is awful. A Gift which simply lets you perfectly predict the weather, including getting a warning when a supernatural change in the weather is about to happen (Vampire, Mage, Werewolf etc. are about to start a giant storm with magic) would be far better and easily fit at Rank 2.<br /> <br /> A good Storyteller could work hard to make this Gift useful for you, but that to me isn't high praise. This is one of those Gifts which would be very handy IRL (especially for a farmer, gardener, hiker, explorer etc.) but doesn't work well in a TTRPG.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Pull of the Chosen Land (3):</b></u></span> You can always find the shortest and fastest route back to the land you protect. This would work, for example, in Malfeas or a Pentex blacksite, leading you to the nearest exit, and from there back home. Naturally the shortest and fastest route won't always be the safest or smartest, but &quot;fastest&quot; will probably guide you towards a viable exit instead of something non-viable, and thus extremely slow.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately this has a Gnosis cost, and a roll, which is just unnecessary. Many Gifts could just remove their cost and/or their roll and be just fine. Still, you pay 1 Gnosis and you roll Perception + Primal-Urge (difficulty 7). On a success the Gift works.<br /> <br /> This is... handy. Especially in weird locations PCs end up in. There are many situations in Werewolf where you can reach a location by some means (Pentex van, Umbral storm, dam explosion, forcibly teleported, went through a portal that closed) and end up in a place where you don't know the way back. I'd prefer this at Rank 1, absolutely, but it's just fine here too.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Shelter of the Earth (3):</b></u></span> With cover, and slow movement, and by avoiding any 'sudden actions', you can become concealed. This costs Gnosis and has a roll, Dexterity + Stealth difficulty 4, and at that point you might as well remove the roll. But still, this is a sort of invisibility power, and that's nice.<br /> <br /> The Gnosis cost, and the reliance on cover (veils, falling leaves, trees, come to mind) are a bit annoying. Personally I would make the Gift cost no Gnosis if you're using a natural source of cover (thick mist, rainfall, a burrow) and only cost Gnosis if you're using a manmade form of cover (privacy screens, a wardrobe). All in all this is handy enough, good for ambushes, good for hiding, though there are better invisibility powers out there.<br /> <hr /><br /> What a woeful list. A bunch of Ambers, almost no Green. I feel like Gurahl deserved a lot better from their Breed lists in all honesty; they don't even get Tribe-specific Gifts, despite being split up into &quot;bear type&quot;. But the good thing is that they move through their Auspices freely... the issue there, however, is that the Auspice lists are <i>also </i>pretty small.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-13T07:01:27-07:00","id":1550098,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Arcas Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Typically the first Auspice a Gurahl experiences, you'll move past this when you leave your mentor's presence and begin working alone more, even when staying in touch with your mentor. Returning to this Auspice later might relate to you realizing that the fool, trickster, cub etc. have the wisdom of knowing that they know nothing. Arcas is associated with the New Moon and is akin to Ragabash of course. Why they didn't just go <u>&quot;use the Ragabash list&quot;</u> I'm not sure.<br /> <br /> Since you're going to start here, you'll probably only pick up Rank 1 Gifts for 3xp, and from then on you'll be paying 10/15/20/25xp, or, 6/9/12/15 if you circle back to Arcas.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Open Seal</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Pulse of the Prey</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Trackless Waste</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Favour of Ursa Minor (Luna's Blessing)</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Walk Like a Man (1):</b></u></span> For no cost, with a difficulty 7 Dexterity + Larceny, you can leave behind your choice of human footprints or bear prints, regardless of what form you're in.<br /> <br /> This is a nice QOL upgrade which could easily be &quot;always on&quot; with no roll. Gurahl being able to leave human footprints in their Crinos form, for example, would be really neat. All in all this is handy and worth getting. Presumably if a Garou or Corax copied it over you'd be leaving wolf or bird prints.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Cajole (2):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Charisma + Empathy, and on a success you can get an &quot;individual&quot; (presumably includes animals, spirits, Fera and humans) to give you something. With 5 successes you can apparently get a human to give you their house.<br /> <br /> We don't get any details on how Veil-compliant this is, or whether the spell 'breaks' afterwards, like &quot;Jesus Christ why the hell did I just give you my house I need somewhere to LIVE&quot;, but all the same this is very powerful. It's unusually on-the-nose mind control for WTA with a very broad list of uses, akin to Dominate 2 from VTM. I love Garou, but the WoD would be in a much worse state if Ragabash got this, I won't lie.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Safe Passage (3):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Dexterity + Primal-Urge (difficulty 6), 1 success lets you leave no traces of your passage for a scene, every additional success lets you include a +1, so with 5 successes you could guide 5 people through a snowy meadow without leaving any footprints.<br /> <br /> The trouble is that this lasts for a scene, not &quot;12 hours&quot; or whatever, and it costs Gnosis. The fact that you could get 1 success and fail to include your allies also really sucks, though given that you're a giant bear monster it's believable that you could carry them all on your back!<br /> <br /> For Gurahl I'd say this is Amber, but keep in mind that there are Gifts and Fetishes which do this job far better. Spending 9/15xp for this could be <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Red </b></span>in plenty of games.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Rejuvenate (4):</b></u></span> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Breath of the Wyld</b></span>, a Level 1 Black Fury Gift, but instead of lasting for a scene it lasts for (Succour) days. +1 die to all mental rolls for, say, 7 days is basically akin to having +1 to Intelligence, Wits and Perception. The +1 difficulty to Rage rolls is also very good for a Gurahl since it takes the odds of you frenzying purely from anger from &quot;unlikely&quot; to &quot;basically non-existant&quot;, which makes you much more reliable arguably.<br /> <br /> What does +1 to mental stats tend to get you? Well you get +1 to the dice rolls, not the stats themselves, which is an important distinction sometimes. If you have any Gifts that key off of Wits, Intelligence or Perception they'll benefit somewhat from this, especially at low difficulties.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Natural State (5):</b></u></span> This Gift is meant to act as a 'reset' button, restoring an area to how it was 'naturally'. This is an interesting thing, by the way. Does a farmhouse return to a meadow? Does it return to the forest which was cut down to make way for the meadow? Does it return to whatever came before the forest?<br /> <br /> Still, you spend 1 Gnosis, and you make a difficulty 8 Manipulation + Primal-Urge.<br /> <br /> I hate Level 5 Gifts thinking they need high difficulty rolls. The difficult thing about doing this is that you need to be Rank 5, and you need to spend a bunch of xp and then some Gnosis. Making it difficulty 8 doesn't make me think &quot;wow, this must be BIG!&quot; it makes me think &quot;what a pain, gonna waste some Gnosis getting this to actually work&quot;. Arguably high Level Gifts should have low difficulty for their activation rolls since that makes them more powerful and coveted. But anyways...<br /> <br /> More successes means a the reversion to nature is more &quot;absolute&quot;. So with 1 success you might fill a whole building with branches and moss, as a new glade starts to choke the structure, but with 5 successes the whole building might be torn apart by trees and disappear into thin air, along with all its contents. The results are very much in the hands of the ST, but the description of the power is very impressive.<br /> <br /> The area you cover is Honour x 20 yards. So with an Honour of 8, say, you'd cover 180 yards as a radius, or 0.1 miles ish. Go on an interactive online map which lets you draw a 0.1 mile radius around, say, your local train station, or airport, or whatever.<br /> <br /> Yep. This is strong. It's awkward to use, but it's very strong. Being able to just destroy a Pentex installation is great, even if you get middling results with 2 successes, for all intents and purposes you have destroyed the building. I'd argue that difficulty 6 would be just fine, but the Gift works as is, and it's a suitably powerful 'epic' Level 5 Power.<br /> <hr /><br /> Honestly this list isn't awful, it just needs like... ~5 more Gifts. It's too small. And yeah, they kept the lists small because Gurahl move between them and can gradually learn everything they want, but they really overdid it with making the lists tiny. Grab a few Ragabash Gifts, say 2 Greens and 3 Ambers, and add them on IMO.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-15T04:19:44-07:00","id":1550146,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Uzmati Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> This is the warrior Auspice, tied to the Full Moon, and typically entered for the first time when you wander alone.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Slash of the Death Bear (Razor Claws)</b></span><br /> (No pre-existing Level 2 Gifts)<br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Silver Claws</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Strength of the Bear</b></span> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>(Hero's Stand)</b></span><br /> <br /> On a Gurahl, Hero's Stand is significantly improved. Your WP is amazing, you may well get a difficulty break on using this Gift, and given your Crinos gives you +5 Stamina, and you can spend Rage for Stamina, getting even more Stamina with this Gift will take you from &quot;very, very hard to harm&quot; to &quot;impossible to harm&quot;.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Ultimatuum (1):</b></u></span> Make a contested Willpower roll (Gurahl get a bonus to these), make an ultimatuum, and force someone to choose one result or the other. For example &quot;either fight me or leave&quot;, or &quot;fight me or let him go&quot; or &quot;fight me or give me the locket&quot;.<br /> <br /> Huh. Lots of ways you can use this Gift to socially leverage the fact that you'd win a direct fight. Very Ahroun-y. If this were a Level 3 Ahroun Gift I'd give it Green. Getting it at Rank 1 is amazing.<br /> <br /> The options you offer can't be suicidal or directly harmful, so &quot;fight me or leave&quot; is okay but &quot;drink this poison or jump off that cliff&quot; isn't. You also can't go &quot;fight me or wrestle with me&quot;.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Mangi's Strong Arms (2):</b></u></span> Concentrate for an action, spend a Rage, roll Strength + Primal-Urge. Every success will give +1 damage to your next successful grappling attack. No, wait, that's +1 <i>die </i>of damage. Given that you can spend Rage to boost your Strength, flat-out, this seems pretty lame unfortunately. Yeah, 6 successes on this is equivalent to +6 Strength... but on a single bear-hug. Meanwhile +1 Strength for (Permanent Rage) turns is actually pretty neat.<br /> <br /> This has its uses, don't get me wrong, you could build up to a one-hit-kill, but you aren't going to one-hit-kill with a Grapple, you're going to KO with your claws. When you get into the weeds of a Gurahl using a Grapple to harm, they could indeed use their claws while grappling to inflict Aggravated damage, W20 corebook says Garou can do this so it makes sense for Gurahl too. So yeah, there's a build here. Grapple people, squeeze em hard. But adding dice of damage to a one-off attack isn't great, especially with a cost.<br /> <br /> You'd be better off puting XP into Rage, or Strength, or Brawl.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Threaten (2):</b></u></span> Charisma + Intimidation, difficulty of the target's WP + 2 (max 9), 3+ successes makes them flee in terror for (Honour) turns, less than that inflicts a -1 penalty to all their dice rolls for (Honour) turns.<br /> <br /> This is handy enough. It's no <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>True Fear</b></span>, but it's good. What's really annoying about that is that Gurahl get True Fear in their General list, and True Fear suits them perfectly thanks to their +5 Strength option in Crinos. So yeah, this is pretty useless in context. Could be Amber, but the wider picture makes it Red.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Crush (3):</b></u></span> <i>PSYCHIC DEATH GRIP</i>. Reminded of the Steel Hand Adept from FFG's Force &amp; Destiny, where you basically play a Kung-Fu Jedi. This Gift lets you do Grapple attacks out to a range of (Honour) feet, which is hilariously strong. On first attempt chances are enemies facing you for the first time won't even bother to dodge.<br /> <br /> This has no cost. It's just a thing you can do. Great for grappling lava elementals, for example, or for restraining someone who is otherwise impossible to reach. I love it when Gifts just let you DO something special, no cost, it's just a natural part of you now, it really suits the flavour of what Gifts are.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Delay the Death Bear's Coming (4):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Wits + Occult/Medicine, difficulty 6, each success grants a target +1 Incapacitated health level. Since you can grant yourself bonus health levels by spending a single Rage point, this is better used on allies than yourself. Naturally having additional Incapacitated health levels has its utility limited by the fact that if you're Incapacitated you can't act, but this Gift can very strongly protect someone from death, whether by injuries or disease or poison.<br /> <br /> This isn't a must-have, but a great combat/healing/protector option which suits the Gurahl perfectly. Some of the best-written and themed Gifts are Amber.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Rage of the Mother Bear (5):</b></u></span> Budget Rage actions, except not budget at all, since you're spending 15/25xp on it and you need to be Rank 5 and you're spending Gnosis, not Rage.<br /> <br /> But the idea is that you spend 1 Gnosis, roll Dexterity + Rituals (difficulty 6), and on your next turn you get a number of additional actions equal to your successes. So for example you could get 6 successes, and on your next turn you'd get 6 bonus attacks, dodges, or so on, slotted in at the end of the turn like other bonus actions.<br /> <br /> It's unclear whether your Dexterity or Wits limit you here. Hopefully not. Assuming that, I'm going to say this is a must-have, but it's very annoying how expensive and late it is. If you're a Rank 5 Gurahl you ought to get this (for action economy, which is a hugely important aspect of any combat TTRPG), but that doesn't make it amazing either. Green through circumstance.<br /> <br /> As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23\" data-vbnamecard=\"23\">Saur Ops Specialist</a> points out, since this doesn't use Rage, it's compatible with things which use Gnosis. So for example if you set yourself up for bonus actions with this Gift, you can then activate a Fetish (rolling Gnosis) on the turn you attack.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 04-16-2025, 08:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-15T12:08:58-07:00","id":1550166,"title":"","content":"Rage of the Mother Bear is mostly a must-have because of how Gurahl can fall behind in the action economy otherwise. But with it, and Crinos Strength at 20? Burger time. Also, since you're not spending Rage to get extra actions, you can make multiple double damage strikes with a fang dagger or the like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-16T06:12:38-07:00","id":1550188,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Kojubat Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Akin to Galliard and tied to the Gibbous Moon, Kojubat is typically entered when a Gurahl becomes a mentor, most likely a Buri-Jaan, for the first time.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Beast Speech</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Eyes of the Soul (Scent of the True Form)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Mind to Mind</b></span> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>(Mindspeak)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Shadows by the Firelight</b></span><br /> <br /> A few weird things here. Bears have terrible eyesight, and W20 knows this since Gurahl have great scent and poor vision. So why change a Gift's name from Scent to Eyes? Anyways beyond that Mindspeak is a Level 1 Gift, so learning it at Rank 3 ain't great. I wouldn't pay 9xp for that Gift personally. Learning Scent of the True Form at Rank 2 instead of 1 isn't great but the Gift is still incredible.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Stonesight (1):</b></u></span> This Gift lets you appraise anything made of stone or rock. This helps you to find hidden minerals, fossils, precious items hidden within stones, and would also help you to appraise a magical object which is made from stone or rock.<br /> <br /> Perception + Enigmas difficulty 5, more successes mean more knowledge. If you were to use this on <i>Lia F\u00e1il</i> for instance you'd get a lot of mystical knowledge about how the Fae of Ireland use it to crown their Kings, though whether the Mists would allow you to keep that knowledge I'm unsure.<br /> <br /> For 3xp this is neat enough to be worth grabbing, especially since if you have it you will find uses for it, even if it's just &quot;aha, this is no ordinary pebble, it's a Talen!&quot;.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Song of Terra (2):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Perception + Primal-Urge (difficulty 6), and touch some earth. You hear the most significant event which has taken place in the last (successes) days, within the presence of this earth. So you might hear a conspiracy being plotted in a remote glade by touching the earth there, or you might hear a roaring argument in a Pentex rep's office a few days back by touching the soil in his potted plant.<br /> <br /> This is pretty great. Even if you assume this will only ever work outdoors, and almost never work indoors, the Gift is a great detective tool which will help you find out a lot of useful stuff in a Chronicle.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Mind Sight (3):</b></u></span> Proper mind reading, which is pretty rare in WTA. Spend 1 Gnosis and roll Wits + Empathy, difficulty of the target's Willpower, and you get to &quot;read their mind&quot; for (successes) turns.<br /> <br /> We don't get any clue as to how this would help in a fight, but I imagine any good Storyteller would highly value &quot;knowing everything your target is going to do a second before they do it&quot; in mechanics, helping you with dodges and parries and so on. We're told that this reads surface thoughts however, so if you want to find out about a deep memory, you need to prompt the person to think about it. Great for interrogations, but you can't just read someone's history like a book.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Probe Thoughts (4):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, plus 1 Willpower if you're targeting a supernatural being, then roll Wits + Empathy, difficulty of the target's Willpower. Every success lets you find out one thing they're trying to keep concealed. Worth noting that &quot;trying to keep concealed&quot; could get you a lot of additional info which might be interesting but not directly relevant, like &quot;I am the father of the cub being raised by the Smiths&quot;.<br /> <br /> This is an even better interrogation power, and a typical Philodox would probably be very envious of this ability.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Aversion Therapy (5):</b></u></span> Attach a strong negative emotion to something for a year and a day. The example given is that a thief could be made to feel terror every time they contemplate stealing. This would be great for gently helping a criminal, a gossip, an addict, and so on. The ability to make someone feel strong disgust every time they consider harming a human or bear would be a good way to peacefully disarm a threat, for example, if it were a creature which couldn't otherwise know better (like a feral beast).<br /> <br /> Spend 1 Gnosis (and 1 Willpower if supernatural), roll Wits + Empathy difficulty of the target's Willpower. Nice and easy, 1 success gets the full effect.<br /> <br /> I really like this one, and it could very strongly define a character's role in society and in a story, it's got the right &quot;absolute&quot; quality a Level 5 Gift should have. But I wouldn't call it a must-have that every Rank 5 Gurahl should make sure they pick up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-16T12:10:55-07:00","id":1550198,"title":"","content":"I\u2019m not going to lie, \u2018Bear\u2019 is almost certainly the most inherently cool shifter morph - wolves and big cats are nothing to sniff at, but bears are just HUGE, omnivorous <i>and</i> can stand up on their own hind legs.<br /> <br /> Also, what sensible animal wants to argue with a full grown bear?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-01T07:59:41-07:00","id":1550539,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Kieh Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Tied to the Crescent Moon and akin to Theurges, a Gurahl might become a Kieh the first time they take up the role of Buri-Jaan.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Spirit Speech</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Sense the Unnatural</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Exorcism</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Name the Spirit</b></span><br /> <br /> Getting Sense the Unnatural at Rank 2 is excellent. Name the Spirit arrives slightly late.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Diagnose* (1):</b></u></span> Perform a full medical examination with a glance.<br /> <br /> So, I'm well within my expertise as a doctor of medicine to say that this would be equally important to tending to the sick as Mother's Touch. Actually examining a patient properly takes time, and it involves exhaustively checking things you're 99% sure will be fine, only to occasionally go &quot;wait... I can't hear ANY sounds from his left lung...&quot; when looking over a patient you thought was completely fine. Being able to do a full appraisal of someone's health using the Medicine Knowledge without having to get their shirt off and grab tools and put them in various positions is very, very handy.<br /> <br /> At a glance you might note that a random mook has lung cancer, that a Pentex rep has a rare blood disorder which is being treated but not cured, that a powerful Garou champion will suffer from seizures if exposed to flashing lights. So, if your ST is actually interested in you being Doctor House the werebear, this Gift could be absolutely character-defining. If your ST is forgetful, or lame, or creates undetailed encounters with unfleshed-out characters, this won't do much at all.<br /> <br /> It's also important to note that this Gift gives your character information that you as a player will often have, and will often bleed into character knowledge. For example say a character in your team has been poisoned and is taking Aggravated damage every turn. You, the player, know that they've taken a bunch of Aggravated damage. The character, meanwhile, might not. But a type of benign meta-gaming we all mostly accept is that you'll throw out a Mother's Touch to heal the person, despite you having no IC way to know that they are suffering from damage which is worth you 'wasting' a point of Gnosis on. This Gift basically alleviates that discrepency and gives you a way to have a solid grasp of who needs healing and who doesn't.<br /> <br /> This is Green, but heavily caveated. If you know what you're doing as a player and character, and invest in the Medicine Knowledge in a world where the health of people and animals matters (which a good WTA game should focus on!), this could be incredible. It could also be a flavour tax if your ST makes shallow white-room dnd-like encounters.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Refresh (2):</b></u></span> This is Resist Pain but targeting another, it requires touch, and it costs Gnosis instead of Willpower. Whoever you touch with this Gift can ignore Wound Penalties for the entire 'combat'.<br /> <br /> Given that all Gurahl get Resist Pain (or &quot;Ignore Wounds&quot;) at Rank 1, this is much more useful for helping Kinfolk, Garou/Fera allies and so on.<br /> <br /> That said, there is a very devious application of this Gift. Use it on a BSD or other Wyrm-touched enemy who feeds on their pain, who draws power from it, and you might significantly weaken them. Think Darth Sion in KOTOR 2 - he literally can't live without his pain, the power he draws from it is all that keeps him going.<br /> <br /> All in all this is a solid amber, worth grabbing especially if you have allies who need it or if you're consistently left with Gnosis left over. That said, to be quite honest, if you have time to touch an ally who is suffering from wounds... you'd probably just use Mother's Touch. Yes, you can use this on someone who hasn't been wounded YET, it isn't useless, but in many circumstances you'd be better off just using Mother's Touch! If beneficiaries of this Gift reduced their Soak difficulties by 1 this would be much better.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Spirit Healing (4):</b></u></span> Charisma + Occult, difficulty of the target Spirit's Willpower (so 7-10 in the case of most powerful spirits). If you succeed, you're permitted to spend Gnosis to heal them, at a 1:2 ratio, so 4 Gnosis would restore 8 Essence.<br /> <br /> This is god-awful.<br /> <br /> It would be very easy to make the Gift a simple &quot;spend 1 Gnosis, roll dice, every success restores 2 Essence&quot;. But no. This Gift even forgets that <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Lover's Touch</b></span> (Ease the Fevered Mind) ALREADY heals spirits for you! And that's a General Gurahl Gift.<br /> <br /> This is both weak and redundant. Skip it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Spirit Shape (4):</b></u></span> Disguise yourself as a spirit, in a way which will convince spirits, for days at a time. This costs 1 Gnosis and you have to pass an Appearance + Enigmas (difficulty 7) roll.<br /> <br /> You take on the 'desired' appearance (so perhaps &quot;Bear Spirit&quot;, or &quot;Dolphin Spirit&quot;, or &quot;Electricity Elemental&quot; etc.) for (Wisdom) days. You can also end the Gift when you wish.<br /> <br /> This is a very handy Gift and I'm surprised that Theurges don't get it. Great utility and a powerful way to blend in in the Umbra. Disguising yourself as a Bane so that you can walk around the most hellish places in the Umbra with impugnity, restoring purity and health where it's needed most, would be a great use. Hell, imagine playing some Garou PCs and finding that one of many Banes you encounter in a hellish wyrmscape turns into a human when attacked, declaring themselves an ally who was just disguising themselves as a bane in order to help Gaia. Do the Pack trust this stranger?<br /> <br /> More high-level Gifts need to be like this. Absolute wording, no &quot;if you get 1 success you'll only convince Spirits who don't beat 1 success on a Gnosis roll&quot;, no nonsense, and it lasts for days.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Image of the Sky Bear (5):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis and roll Wits + Rituals, difficulty 6. 7 points of starlight appear around you, giving you 7 'charges' of this Gift to expend.<br /> <br /> A charge can be used to:<br /> <br /> 1) Instantly heal 5 Bashing or Lethal from a target on touch<br /> <br /> 2) Instantly heal 3 Aggravated from a target on touch<br /> <br /> 3) Grant mystical body armour which is equivalent to Kevlar in protection (3 armour)<br /> <br /> It's unclear whether the 'kevlar' option inflicts the -1 Dexterity penalty which is associated with such armour. Presumably not, but ask your ST. If they're cool they'll say &quot;no, it's weightless mystical armour, obviously&quot; though it was silly of the writers to not just make the Gift say &quot;grant +3 soak to the target&quot;.<br /> <br /> For 1 Gnosis you get the ability to heal 21 Aggravated, or 35 Bashing/Lethal, or some combination of those. This is an incredible party healing ability which would let you restore a small crowd from near-death to being fighting fit. This Gift also lets you heal yourself, and remember that RAW werebears don't get Mother's Touch! It also helps you to heal those who would otherwise be very hard to heal with Mother's Touch due to their high Rage.<br /> <br /> Basically in any situation where you're healing people, this Gift is probably superior, unless you have a single turn to heal 4+ points of Aggravated, in which case you should use Mother's Touch and just pray for 4+ successes. A great saving of Gnosis, plus a great way to grant armour to allies. This is among the better Rank 5 Gifts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-01T11:33:35-07:00","id":1550542,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rishi Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Auspice of the Half Moon, Rishi are akin to Philodox. This Auspice is associated with taking up leadership, and is traditionally the final role a Gurahl enters in their life.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Truth of Gaia</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Presence of the Great Bear (King of (the) Beasts)</b></span><br /> (No pre-existing Level 3 Gifts)<br /> (No pre-existing Level 4 Gifts)<br /> <b>Oath of the Great Bear (Geas)</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Befriend (1):</b></u></span> Passive +1 success to making a positive first impression. Neat for 3xp. The ability to make friends more easily is great, though this would be most useful at Rank 1, and it's unlikely you'll be a Rishi at Rank 1 unless you have had a very unusual experience since your First Change.<br /> <br /> Seeming like a good friend to everyone you meet is a nice flavour power, and the positive effect of the Gift will come up semi-frequently.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Compel Truth (2):</b></u></span> Target an individual, spend 1 Gnosis, roll Wits + Empathy (difficulty of the target's Willpower). For every success they can't deliberately speak a falsehood for one minute.<br /> <br /> So, for example, while a person was being questioned in a trial you could rob them of the power to lie, then question them exhaustively over the few minutes this Gift provides. This is an excellent ability which the Philodox would be much improved by.<br /> <br /> Remember that someone under the effects of this Gift can remain silent, unless you were to compel them to speak with some other ability.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Waken* (3):</b></u></span> I really like that a thunder-spirit teaches this!<br /> <br /> End any kind of unnatural or supernatural sleep. A sleeping curse, a Mummy awaiting resurrection, a Vampire in torpor, a werebear in hibernation and other such slumbers can be ended with this Gift. Simply spend 1 Gnosis and roll Wits + Rituals, difficulty 7.<br /> <br /> To my taste the roll is unnecessary. A Gift this niche should just work. This Gift isn't amazing or anything, but it works well for what it does, and it has a nice absoluteness. I don't know why you'd want to take a Vampire out of torpor, but being able to awaken other Gurahl is handy. All in all I'd call this a Red, but it could be useful and worth purchasing in some games. Honestly I'd rather rely on NPCs for this if the story permits.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Ways of the Tapestry* (3):</b></u></span> Flavour wise this lets you understand the universe through strange hints, getting an understanding of the 'tapestry' of the Triat. Roll Wits + Enigmas, difficulty 8, no cost, and every success gives you an &quot;oblique but helpful&quot; clue about some problem you're facing.<br /> <br /> This really depends on the temperament of your ST, but RAW it's meant to be helpful, and the lack of cost is very handy. I'd say that Werespiders really, really ought to have this, along with several Werecat Tribes, and that the difficulty of 8 is annoying... so it's hard to rate this Green at any rate.<br /> <br /> Assuming a sensible ST this is a solid Amber. Might be well worth grabbing, but not an auto-take or must-have.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Bestow Ursa's Blessing (4):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, describe a blessing you want to impart, roll Wits + Occult (difficulty 7), and if you succeed the target gets a blessing for (Wisdom) hours.<br /> <br /> That blessing will boil down to automatically succeeding at a pre-determined task (no examples given unfortunately), or +1 to an Attribute.<br /> <br /> So, for example, a Gurahl using this Gift on a Garou ally might describe how they will sneak into the inner sanctum of a great servant of the Wyrm, and that might manifest as them automatically succeeding at some important test they must pass to get there, or in them having +1 Dexterity for (Wisdom) hours, for example.<br /> <br /> This is flexible and interesting, and you have plenty of Gnosis to spend, so it's a handy enough Gift. This is more of a Support power for when you're sending people on quests, and the system isn't really built for stories where you're the quest-giver per se. If you're going on the adventure personally this is a great ability to buff your allies, especially since +1 Dexterity to a multi-action ally is amazing and +1 Stamina could be huge if you're being attacked 10 times in a single round. The ability to boost an ally's crafting / Ritual potential is also neat. So all things considered I do need to give this a Green, but it really does rely on you making good use of it and finding the right synergies.<br /> <br /> Narratively speaking you can sidestep some problems entirely as well. You could describe how Mother Bear's blessings will guide a mother-to-be through her labour without fail, and entirely avoid problems of a difficult birth. This Gift could do a million different moderately impressive things, which is pretty neat.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Quell Mob Rage (4):</b></u></span> This being taught by a manatee-spirit feels almost like a meme. Yeah, those dudes look pretty chill. Is that it? I mean arguably a dove or a capybara could teach this too right, by that metric?<br /> <br /> Anyways, this is pretty neat. You can make a lynch mob or similar calm down and start to disperse, non-violently, which is a great move since even for a werecreature a giant crowd is one of those problems you can't just murder your way past, at least not in this 'age'.<br /> <br /> Roll Charisma + Leadership, difficulty of the highest Willpower rating in the crowd. More successes means a bigger impact on the crowd's emotions (so 1 success will have a mild effect, 5 successes would have an extreme effect, presumably), and you effect Succour x 30 people.<br /> <br /> I'll be honest, at Rank 4 I expect a Calm Crowd Gift to just Calm Crowds on a success. What do you mean more successes means a more dramatic impact? This Gift cost me 12xp! This could be worth grabbing but it's a bit of a weird one, and when a crowd has good reason to be angry it starts to get into weirder territory. Letting cooler heads prevail in the morning after averting a momentary riot is great, the power is handy, but within its niche it doesn't quite hit the nail on the head, especially since the difficulty might be very, very high if there's a single supernatural in the crowd with good Willpower, or just a headstrong human.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-01T16:09:20-07:00","id":1550557,"title":"","content":"Image of the Sky Bear - you get the healing capabilities of Kenshiro. You're going to need to swing hard if you want enemies to blow up the same way, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-02T03:07:42-07:00","id":1550570,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rites</b></u><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The werebears also know most of the rites common to Garou, having taught many of them to the werewolves to begin with. </div> </div> </div>Ouch!<br /> <br /> <u><b>Dreams of Winter</b></u><br /> <br /> A simple Rite you can set up before you sleep or hibernate, which gives you visions of things that take place during your slumber. This means you might awaken from a year's sleep, or a night's, fully aware of important events you need to know. You tend to see stuff which is local (in your territory) or very relevant to you. The less relevant something is, the harder it is to see, which is reflected in the difficulty of the dice roll.<br /> <br /> This is excellent, easy to learn, and basically allows you to encounter information you'd otherwise really struggle to come by. For example as a Gurahl you might awaken from your slumber with dreams of Garou having moved into your territory and already showing signs of high aggression against your Kinfolk; that information could absolutely save your life!<br /> <br /> <u><b>Rite of Rending the Gauntlet</b></u><br /> <br /> Enter Bjornen form and physically tear a hole into the Gauntlet. Only Gurahl can use this doorway, but essentially with the existence of this Rite Gurahl get to 'step sideways' freely.<br /> <br /> You have to spend 1 Gnosis to do this, but you get to roll Charisma + Rituals instead of Gnosis, vs the local Gauntlet, so you might have much better luck getting into the Umbra than a random Garou when you use this Rite. In places where the Gauntlet is very strong you might find ways of boosting your Charisma with a Gift or Rite or Fetish, or adjusting the difficulty.<br /> <br /> With 3 successes you get through the Gauntlet instantly! Pretty good going and a must-have for a Gurahl, even if you have the power to Step Sideways via some Fetish or Merit or Gift.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Rite of Replenishment</b></u><br /> <br /> This is a Gnosis restoration Rite which you can use in any place which is suitably close to Gaia, so long as you don't repeat the Rite in the same place more than once a month. With a Gift like this, who needs Caerns? Excellent Rite, but I find myself questioning the Gnosis economy given how effective this Rite is.<br /> <br /> If you chant and sing and dance for an hour, this Rite is base difficulty 4. Wits + Rituals difficulty 4, every success grants a point of Gnosis, and while the Gnosis does have to be distributed among the participants, that's still a lot of Gnosis! The main thing to keep in mind here is that this is not a Mystic Rite, and gathering 3+ Gurahl to perform this Rite would be hard. Essentially if you have a Gurahl community you have nigh-limitless Gnosis restoration and if you don't, you don't. Makes the push to restore the lost numbers of the werebears more pressing.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Rite of the River-Portent</b></u><br /> <br /> Read the future using the guts of a fish you've just pulled from a river. Pretty standard.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Rite of True Mating</b></u><br /> <br /> Create a talisman which pulls you towards a suitable Kinfolk mate. This Rite might help you to find one Kinfolk in a city where you otherwise could have found none, so immediately it's pretty handy! It will default to someone who is fertile and would suit you as a mate to produce more Gurahl with, it's entirely practical, but if you're looking for love and you're compatible with the opposite sex this could work quite well.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Rite of Fighting the Death Bear</b></u><br /> <br /> Fight Mangi, the Death Bear, and if you win you can reclaim someone who has died. This doesn't cost permanent Gnosis and you can do it at any Rank. Mangi is supposed to be statted in a way where he will &quot;rarely&quot; be beaten by mid-ranked Gurahl. If this Rite is one you feel comfortable and confident calling upon, your Storyteller is going too easy on you!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}